 "script": [
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "In eighteen fifty-eight, London smelled so bad, the government almost ran away from its own building."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "And no, I'm not exaggerating. They called it the Great Stink."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Here's the deal. Two and a half million people, and almost all their toilet waste went straight into one river. The Thames."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "The same river a lot of them drank from."
  },
  {
   "beat": "heat",
   "text": "Then came a brutal heatwave, and the river started to cook."
  },
  {
   "beat": "heat",
   "text": "Sailors gagged. People crossed the bridges with rags over their noses."
  },
  {
   "beat": "heat",
   "text": "And inside Parliament, right on the river, they soaked the curtains in chemicals, just to breathe."
  },
  {
   "beat": "fear",
   "text": "And you know what scared them most? They thought the smell itself was killing people."
  },
  {
   "beat": "fear",
   "text": "Wrong. The real killer was in the water. Cholera. A few years earlier, it killed over ten thousand Londoners."
  },
  {
   "beat": "fix",
   "text": "But that smell finally got them moving. They rushed a new law through in about eighteen days. Eighteen!"
  },
  {
   "beat": "fix",
   "text": "An engineer named Joseph Bazalgette built over a thousand miles of sewers, and made the pipes way bigger than he needed. Just in case."
  },
  {
   "beat": "fix",
   "text": "Good call. London still uses them today."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "You get it? I said, you get it? A bad smell fixed a whole city."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "That's real. That's about it, folks. Follow for more strange history. See ya!"
  }
 ],
 "science": [
  {
   "claim": "Summer 1858: a heatwave turned the Thames, which carried most of London's sewage, into an open stink; Parliament (on the river) hung curtains soaked in chloride of lime and considered moving; a bill to fund new sewers passed in about 18 days; Joseph Bazalgette's system (about 82 miles of intercepting sewers and some 1,100 miles of street sewers) is still in use, and he famously enlarged the pipe sizes as a margin of safety; London's population was about 2.5 million; the 1853-54 cholera outbreak killed over 10,000 Londoners; people believed in miasma (bad air), while John Snow had linked cholera to water in 1854.",
   "working": "",
   "source": "Wikipedia, Great Stink; Halliday, The Great Stink of London (1999); Science Museum; Museum of London"
  }
 ],
